WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.750 --> 00:00:02.820
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:07.050 --> 00:00:09.450
NARRATOR: Food trucks used
to only sell sandwiches

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.580
and other prepackaged food.

00:00:11.580 --> 00:00:13.320
Not anymore.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.630
The modern food truck
is a mobile restaurant

00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:16.985
with a full kitchen inside.

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:20.970
Food is cooked fresh
and sold to customers

00:00:20.970 --> 00:00:22.130
through a service window.

00:00:24.902 --> 00:00:26.750
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:29.630
Most food trucks are
standard panel trucks.

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.820
Their cargo area
is a fully equipped

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:34.970
commercial kitchen with a
service window on one side.

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:41.960
They're usually customized
by boutique companies,

00:00:41.960 --> 00:00:43.550
so there's no standard method.

00:00:45.810 --> 00:00:48.660
However, certain modifications
to the panel trucks

00:00:48.660 --> 00:00:52.080
are common, like enhancing
the truck's suspension system

00:00:52.080 --> 00:00:54.870
or reinforcing the flooring
and walls to support

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:55.770
the kitchen's weight.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:59.430
To lighten the
load, this company

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.910
builds the kitchen's structural
components out of aluminum bars

00:01:02.910 --> 00:01:04.230
rather than heavy steel.

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:10.600
Once the pieces are
cut, a worker assembles,

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:11.575
then welds them together.

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:17.780
This structure will support
a stainless-steel countertop.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.985
Workers cut the countertops
and cabinets from thick sheets

00:01:28.985 --> 00:01:29.785
of stainless steel.

00:01:35.370 --> 00:01:38.120
They bend the sheets using a
machine called a press break.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:47.590
For this countertop, they
bend a 90-degree angle

00:01:47.590 --> 00:01:51.250
on the front end to form
a 1.5 inch square edge.

00:01:54.070 --> 00:01:56.290
To construct walls
and ceilings, they

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.960
rivet thin sheets of
stainless steel or aluminum

00:01:58.960 --> 00:01:59.980
to panels of wood.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:05.425
Fire-retardant plywood
is used for the kitchens.

00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:09.699
The boards are installed
over Styrofoam insulation.

00:02:11.620 --> 00:02:15.230
They leave the truck's
aluminum floor as is.

00:02:15.230 --> 00:02:18.740
Once the walls, ceiling,
and flooring are complete,

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:20.380
it's time to install
the kitchen's

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.310
cabinets and counters.

00:02:24.430 --> 00:02:26.200
Workers screw each
counter support

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:28.810
to the wooden
structure of the wall,

00:02:28.810 --> 00:02:30.715
then fasten the
countertop with rivets.

00:02:39.700 --> 00:02:41.920
They peel off the film
that was protecting

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:43.045
the stainless-steel surface.

00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:49.300
Next, they install
the major appliances,

00:02:49.300 --> 00:02:51.340
which include a
commercial refrigerator,

00:02:51.340 --> 00:02:53.474
freezer, and cooking equipment.

00:02:56.140 --> 00:02:58.930
Then the electrical
box is installed.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:01.060
They connect wiring
for the outlets,

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:03.250
lighting, water
heater, water pump,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:04.570
and air conditioning system.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.710
The generator is installed
underneath the nearest counter.

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:18.830
It supplies the truck
with electricity.

00:03:18.830 --> 00:03:21.695
Next to the electrical box
is the power transfer box.

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:25.100
It contains a switch
that enables the truck

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.740
to use an outdoor power source.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.530
The switches for the interior
and exterior lighting

00:03:30.530 --> 00:03:32.045
are underneath the
electrical box.

00:03:38.590 --> 00:03:41.890
Workers install the light
fixtures, cabinetry, sink,

00:03:41.890 --> 00:03:42.745
and serving window.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:50.170
They also install the
gas lines that power the

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:51.925
commercial cooking equipment.

00:03:53.230 --> 00:03:55.960
This truck has several
gas appliances--

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.960
a six burner gas
range with large oven,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:02.770
a deep fryer, a charcoal grill,
and a flattop cooking surface.

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:08.200
The kitchen is equipped
with a ventilation

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.520
hood over the cooking area.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.020
Its powerful fan gathers
smoke and sends it

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:15.580
out a vent on top of the truck.

00:04:17.589 --> 00:04:19.180
It's time to outfit
the exterior.

00:04:23.680 --> 00:04:26.290
Some food trucks are
painted, but most

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:28.915
use graphics that are digitally
printed on adhesive vinyl.

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:36.910
After positioning each
panel of the design,

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.365
they apply heat with a torch.

00:04:40.300 --> 00:04:43.180
This removes trapped air,
stretching the vinyl over

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:44.800
rivets and other protrusions.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:55.240
This mobile eatery
is ready to roll

00:04:55.240 --> 00:04:57.130
with a kitchen that
meets fire code

00:04:57.130 --> 00:04:59.050
and food service regulations.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:04.870
It may not provide the ambiance
of a sit-down restaurant,

00:05:04.870 --> 00:05:09.690
but when a food truck pulls up,
you never need a reservation.

